  "change" means the task became due, or a reminder fired. These produce genuinely different systems, and picking wrong
  means building the wrong half.

  - [1mMulti-user collaboration[0m — notifications exist because someone else acted. Requires accounts, auth, a shared server,
    per-task subscription, and an event log. This is the large version: notifications can't be simpler than the
    collaboration model underneath them, and that model is most of the work.
  - [1mSingle-user reminders[0m — notifications are time-driven: due dates, nudges. No accounts, no server strictly required;
    a local app with scheduled notifications covers it. Dramatically smaller — and "who cares about this task" collapses
    to "you do."
  - [1mBoth, collaboration first[0m — same as the first, with time-based reminders as a later addition riding the same
    delivery path.

  Cheap to change later: delivery channels, digest batching, notification copy. Expensive to change later: whether there
  are accounts and a server at all — that's the decision that either does or doesn't force auth, persistence, and a
  backend into existence, and retrofitting multi-user onto a single-user local app is close to a rewrite.

  My recommendation: tell me which of these you actually mean rather than letting me infer it from the word "users." If
  it's genuinely collaborative, I'd rather we design the task and sharing model deliberately than have notifications
  drag it in sideways.
